---
// design/04_LICENSING.md, ui_ux/04_LICENSING_UI.md.
//
// HARD RULES PANEL, a real source conflict, resolved not silently: ui_ux/04 §2 gives five
// specific bracketed rule lines for this panel, one of which ("No delivery-date commitments")
// does not appear in design/04 §Section 4 at all — design/04's own fifth item there is
// "On-premise only." Both are real, sourced commitments ("No delivery-date commitments" is
// design/13's own Never-Permitted rule). Read this as two different things rather than a
// contradiction: ui_ux/04's five lines are the literal content for this specific terse panel
// component (which design/04 doesn't itself describe as a panel), while design/04's own five
// numbered items are reproduced below as the fuller prose section, headers and all. Nothing
// from either source is dropped.
//
// HOVER: the dim-others-on-hover interaction is pure CSS (:hover + sibling selector) — it
// needs no JavaScript, so it is built now rather than deferred to Block 4.
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import Eyebrow from '../components/Eyebrow.astro';
import Button from '../components/Button.astro';

const layers = [
	{
		id: 'l0',
		name: 'L0 · THE PRIMITIVE',
		status: 'MIT, forever. Free.',
		body: 'Contains: the core ORMAS engine, telemetry channels, the submitted paper, and 383 experimental runs. For: researchers, academics, independent evaluators. Purpose: credibility, peer review, and standard-setting. Revenue: zero.',
	},
	{
		id: 'l1',
		name: 'L1 · THE ARTIFACT LAYER',
		status: 'Source-available, non-compete, date-certain conversion to permissive.',
		body: 'Free for academic/non-commercial research. Paid for regulated commercial deployment. Contains: the report generator, the diff engine, and artifact signing mechanisms. Purpose: the format play — wide reading, controlled commercial use.',
	},
	{
		id: 'l2',
		name: 'L2 · THE DEPLOYMENT LICENCE',
		status: 'Commercial, annual, on-premise per-institution.',
		body: 'All revenue lives here. Contains: integration bindings, the calibrated bound for the customer\'s specific domain, version-pinned reproducible builds, support, and indemnity. Purpose: enterprise execution and liability transfer.',
	},
	{
		id: 'l3',
		name: 'L3 · THE ASSURANCE SERVICE',
		status: 'Engagement-priced.',
		body: 'Contains: bound calibration for novel domains, change-control plan drafting, expert testimony. Purpose: custom regulatory alignment and validation defense. It makes L2 renew.',
	},
];

const hardRules = [
	'No per-seat pricing',
	'No usage metering that phones home',
	'No free tier that touches regulated data',
	'No open-core bait-and-switch on L0',
	'No delivery-date commitments',
];
---

<Page title="LICENSING">
	<section class="section container">
		<Eyebrow>LICENSING</Eyebrow>
		<h1>The engine is MIT-licensed. The record it generates belongs to you.</h1>
		<p class="licensing-sub">
			The commercial structure of ORMAS separates the public mechanism of validation from
			the private execution of compliance. The commercial value is the record.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section container">
		<h2>Four layers. All revenue from one.</h2>
		<div class="licensing-stack">
			{
				layers.slice().reverse().map((layer) => (
					<div class={`licensing-band licensing-band--${layer.id}`}>
						<p class="licensing-band-name">{layer.name}</p>
						<p class="licensing-band-status">{layer.status}</p>
						<p class="licensing-band-body">{layer.body}</p>
					</div>
				))
			}
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section container">
		<h2>The engine is free. The record is yours.</h2>
		<p class="licensing-prose">
			The open-source engine creates the market, but the compliance record it generates for
			your infrastructure is the solution. When you run ORMAS on your training run, the
			flight recorder that results belongs to you. Your infrastructure. Your audit trail.
			Your compliance evidence. That record cannot be replicated without running ORMAS. The
			code is free. The record is yours.
		</p>
		<div class="licensing-clause">
			<p class="licensing-clause-label">Artifact Survival Clause</p>
			<p class="licensing-prose">
				On termination of the contract, the customer retains the perpetual, irrevocable
				right to read, retain, and reproduce every artifact generated during the term,
				alongside a frozen reader binary. Your regulatory obligations outlive our
				relationship; your access to your audit trail must do the same.
			</p>
		</div>
		<div class="licensing-clause">
			<p class="licensing-clause-label">Bound Immutability Clause</p>
			<p class="licensing-prose">
				The declared bound and modification set are fixed at execution. They may only be
				altered through a documented, mutually signed amendment that generates its own
				permanent artifact. The bound cannot be silently changed. If we can silently change
				the bound, the bound is worthless.
			</p>
		</div>
		<div class="licensing-clause">
			<p class="licensing-clause-label">Reproducible Build Attestation</p>
			<p class="licensing-prose">
				The customer receives hash-pinned builds. You possess the cryptographic means to
				verify that the binary operating in your environment is the exact artifact that
				was validated and insured.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section container">
		<h2>Pre-booking vs. post-launch availability</h2>
		<p class="licensing-prose">
			Prior to General Availability, terms are negotiable. This is the only window.
			Institutions that commit during this engineering validation phase become Charter
			Partners. Charter Partners lock in flat-rate, enterprise-wide pricing for 5 years. They
			experience no consumption metering and gain direct access to the engineering team.
		</p>
		<p class="licensing-prose">
			Following General Availability, pricing is published and fixed. There is no individual
			negotiation, and there are no exceptions. The pricing model shifts strictly to
			per-model consumption rates.
		</p>
		<p class="licensing-prose">
			Institutional buyers require budget predictability over multi-year horizons; we
			require strict engineering capacity commitments. Flexible pricing post-launch creates
			an unbounded negotiation overhead that a capacity-constrained infrastructure team
			cannot absorb.
		</p>
		<Button variant="primary" href="/contact">Discuss pre-booking terms</Button>
	</section>

	<section class="section container">
		<h2>The on-premise requirement</h2>
		<p class="licensing-prose">
			Every ORMAS commercial deployment is on-premise. We do not offer a managed cloud
			service. Regulated environments — including hospital enclaves governed by HIPAA,
			European entities subject to GDPR Article 5, and classified defense networks — require
			absolute data sovereignty. Sending neural network telemetry or internal model weights
			to an external SaaS provider is a disqualifying security risk.
		</p>
		<p class="licensing-prose">
			Integration occurs entirely within the customer's secure environment. The ORMAS binary
			operates where your data lives. We do not see your data.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section container">
		<h2>The L0 commitment</h2>
		<p class="licensing-prose">
			The core ORMAS engine is released under the MIT licence. This is a permanent
			commitment, not a temporary default. Reproducibility is the foundational sales asset.
			In markets that purchase based on auditability, a closed reimplementation of a
			published method is strictly less credible than the published mechanism itself.
		</p>
		<p class="licensing-prose licensing-moat">
			The moat is not the engine. Any competent engineering team can fork the repository.
			The moat is what you produce by running it. The compliance record your infrastructure
			generates belongs to you, and it cannot be replicated without running ORMAS.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section container">
		<div class="licensing-hard-rules">
			{
				hardRules.map((rule) => (
					<p class="licensing-hard-rule">
						<span class="licensing-hard-rule-mark">[ ✗ ]</span> {rule}
					</p>
				))
			}
		</div>
	</section>
</Page>
